4030 Belt Line Rd.            2246 Guadalupe St.
Irving, TX  75038             Austin, TX 78705
(214)258-8324                 (512)476-7211 ext.394

MATT'S TALK

Here are Matt's notes about his talk and demo:

"I'll cover the "new and hot" features of Linux, those features
which make Linux stand apart from other systems and prove that 
it's an excellent platform for experimental development of all 
sorts. Linux is still very much a dynamic operating system, 
although the "core UNIX" functionality is very much in place, 
that doesn't mean it's slowing down.

"I'd like to talk in technical terms about the implementations of
several new projects being done under Linux. This will help the 
audience understand what's involved in adding new features to the
system, and maybe encourage others to get involved. I'd like to
cover the implementations of:
        * iBCS2 support (allowing SVR4, SCO, etc. binaries to
          run under Linux);
        * ELF support (AT&T's Executable and Linkable Format,
          which defines the format for object files, executables,
          and libraries used by the system);
        * WINE (The MS-Windows Emulator being developed under
          Linux systems);
        * Symmetric Multiprocessing and Kernel Threads support 
          (allowing multiprocessor machines to operate under
          Linux, as well as full kernel-level threading);
        * Support for ATM Network Interfaces (a new technology
          for networks, allowing high-speed communications over
          optical fibre);
        * Other projects of note---this might include my own
          work on supporting "real time" video over ATM networks,
          using a Linux machine equipped with PCI-bus framegrabber
          and ATM network interface, or Don Becker's "Beowulf"
          project to run a large network of fast-Ethernetted Linux
          machines as a virtual parallel machine.

"The talk will describe the status of these projects but will 
concentrate on the implementation details. It will be at a technical
level that people who are familiar with simply using Linux systems
will appreciate, as well as those with more programming or UNIX 
design background."

OTHER FUN STUFF

     In support of Matt's "Running on Linux" tour we will make
available at each tour stop our new Linux t-shirts and camp mugs.
If you have any questions, please contact the tour stop location
or send mail to Christina Silveira at cms@ora.com.
     Please do spread the word.  We're hoping that Matt's tour
invites a lot more people to begin developing and using Linux.

--Brian 

